## Code adapted from MIT projects

### yt-dual-subs

- Source: https://github.com/Gythiro/yt-dual-subs
- License: MIT
- Used for: player runtime timedtext acquisition (`inject`), native caption
  suppression, overlay/SPA lifecycle patterns, BYOK translation channel shape.
- Upstream copyright: see the upstream `LICENSE`; the exact line is carried in
  every adapted file.

### easysubs

- Source: https://github.com/Nitrino/easysubs
- License: MIT
- Used for: sentence-level previous / repeat / next navigation model and the
  YouTube player adapter shape (key mapping is Earworm's `a` / `s` / `d`).
- Upstream copyright: see the upstream `LICENSE`; the exact line is carried in
  every adapted file.

### SceneLingo

- Source: https://github.com/umutgokbulak/SceneLingo
- License: MIT
- Used for: reference for the “meaning of the word in this sentence + phrase
  identification” prompt design. Only used if the prompt text is adapted;
  SceneLingo's own code is not vendored.
- Upstream copyright: see the upstream `LICENSE`; the exact line is carried in
  any adapted file.

## Data

### ECDICT

- Source: https://github.com/skywind3000/ECDICT
- License: the ECDICT repository is MIT-licensed. Some third-party dictionary
  content bundled upstream has a conflicting CC-BY provenance; this is noted
  here for transparency. Earworm ships only a trimmed word list generated by
  `scripts/build-ecdict.ts`.
- Used for: offline English→Chinese dictionary (`public/data/ecdict.json`,
  `public/data/lemmas.json`).

### OpenSubtitles collocations

- Source: https://huggingface.co/datasets/vladvlasov256/opensubs-collocations
- License: CC-BY 4.0.
- Attribution: OpenSubtitles, Lison & Tiedemann 2016.
- Used for: offline bigram collocation table (`public/data/collocations.json`),
